WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.579
Habits that keep you learning.

2
00:00:01.859 --> 00:00:07.405
These follow from the study’s high-scoring patterns and from the verification habits of this elective.

3
00:00:07.685 --> 00:00:09.093
Predict before you read.

4
00:00:09.373 --> 00:00:14.344
Before opening the agent’s diagnosis or fix, write down what you think the cause is.

5
00:00:14.624 --> 00:00:15.584
Then compare.

6
00:00:15.864 --> 00:00:19.597
You’ll learn where your mental model is wrong, and you’ll review more carefully.

7
00:00:19.877 --> 00:00:21.435
Ask why, not just what.

8
00:00:21.715 --> 00:00:31.336
After a change you couldn’t have written yourself, ask for an explanation of the key decision and the alternative it rejected, then check the explanation against the code.

9
00:00:31.616 --> 00:00:34.923
Use learning-oriented modes in areas you want to master.

10
00:00:35.203 --> 00:00:50.691
Claude Code’s built-in Explanatory output style adds short “Insight” notes on the choices behind the code; the Learning style also leaves small pieces of real design decisions (marked TODO(human) ) for you to write, then comments on your code.

11
00:00:50.971 --> 00:00:52.741
Switch with /output-style.

12
00:00:53.021 --> 00:00:58.611
Other tools have similar modes; the study’s authors recommend tool designers build more of them.

13
00:00:58.891 --> 00:01:01.152
Write the first version yourself, sometimes.

14
00:01:01.432 --> 00:01:08.579
For a new library, language, or domain, do the first small task by hand, then compare with what the agent would have done.

15
00:01:08.859 --> 00:01:10.672
Do the debugging when it’s teachable.

16
00:01:10.952 --> 00:01:21.192
When a bug is in a system you’ll own, drive the investigation yourself and use the agent as a fast pair of hands (run this, search that), not as the detective.

17
00:01:21.472 --> 00:01:22.731
Review as practice.

18
00:01:23.011 --> 00:01:27.405
Reading agent code carefully, against a checklist, is itself skill-building.

19
00:01:27.685 --> 00:01:28.731
Skimming isn’t.
